<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Halmahera</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jailolo Island</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Halmahera is the largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, an irregular, four-armed island in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-maluku">North Maluku</a>. The small clove-producing islands of Ternate and Tidore lie off its west coast, and the sultanates of Ternate, Tidore and Jailolo competed for control of its coasts, people and sago supplies. It saw heavy fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/halmahera.jpg" alt="Halmahera" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muhammad ECTOR Prasetyo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corner_of_Halmahera_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Halmahera, formerly known as Jilolo, Gilolo or Jailolo, has a land area of 17,780 square kilometres and is the seventh largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It is a volcanic island on an island arc that includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ampat-islands">Raja Ampat Islands</a>, all uplifted by the northward movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the subduction of the Pacific Plate. Dukono is an active volcano at the northern end of the island and Mount Ibu another on its northwest coast. Ultramafic rocks and ophiolites form the bedrock over large areas of the eastern peninsula. The flightless invisible rail and the palm Jailoloa halmaherensis are found only on the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sparsely populated Halmahera has long had its fortunes tied to those of the smaller islands of Ternate and Tidore off its west coast. It was the site of the Sultanate of Jailolo, one of the four kingdoms of Maluku, the Maloko Kië Raha, with Ternate, Tidore and Bacan, in the era before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> colonized the whole archipelago. The north of the island was the base of the Loloda Kingdom, one of the earlier Moluccan kingdoms of the 13th century, though it was not counted among the four prominent ones.</p><p>The naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace">Alfred Russel Wallace</a> visited Halmahera, as he described in his book The Malay Archipelago (1869). In February 1858, on Ternate or perhaps on Halmahera itself, between bouts of fever, he arrived at the idea of natural selection, wrote it down over the next couple of days and sent the letter to Darwin.</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the island was the site of a Japanese naval base at Kao Bay. In 1999 and 2000 a dispute between the mainly Christian district of Kao and the wholly Muslim district of Malifut grew into religious fighting that spread through much of the North Moluccas, and thousands of people on Halmahera were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sofifi, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-maluku">North Maluku</a> province, stands on the west coast of Halmahera, opposite Tidore; the provincial government moved there from Ternate in 2010. In that year the island itself had a population of 449,938, and about half of its inhabitants are Muslim and half Christian. Tobelo is the largest town. The coast is inhabited by the Tobelo people, while the Togutil, a small semi-nomadic related people of the interior, are estimated at 1,500 to 3,000 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Halmahera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jailolo on the west coast of <b>Halmahera</b> was one of the four Moluccan kingdoms, with Ternate, Tidore and Bacan, known as the Moloku Kie Raha.</blockquote><a href="/state/jailolo-sultanate">Jailolo Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Ternate, a small volcanic island off <b>Halmahera</b>, was one of the few places in the world where cloves grew.</blockquote><a href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Tidore's wealth came from cloves, and its sultans claimed overlordship of southern <b>Halmahera</b>, the Raja Ampat islands and coasts of New Guinea, which sent tribute.</blockquote><a href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
